## Standard input and standard output.
##
## `print` writes to the same standard output as `write` and
## `write_line`, so all three interleave in the order they ran. Every
## read first writes out whatever has been written so far, so a prompt
## is on screen before the program waits for an answer.
#
# The Pyking half of `io`. What the host provides is declared in
# io_external.pyk beside this file and is in scope here unqualified --
# the two files are one module -- so `prompt_line` below is the host's.
#
# `read_line` is written here rather than declared so that its prompt
# can have a default: a program asks for a line with `io.read_line()`
# and for an answer to a question with `io.read_line("Name? ")`.


## Writes `prompt`, with no newline, then reads one line of standard input.
## The line ending is not part of the answer.
##
## # Parameters
## - `prompt`: what to show before waiting for the line; nothing by
##   default.
##
## # Returns
## The line, or `""` at the end of the input. Use `is_eof` to tell an
## empty line from the end.
##
## # Examples
## ```pyking stdin = "Ada\n"
## import io
## name = io.read_line("Your name: ")
## print("Hello,", name)
## ```
## ```stdout
## Your name: Hello, Ada
## ```
public def read_line(prompt: String = "") -> String:
    return prompt_line(prompt)
